<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- Reading the owner's audio files, and nothing else.

         Android 13 (API 33) split the single storage permission into per-media
         ones. READ_MEDIA_AUDIO is the one a music player wants: it asks for
         exactly what this application reads and gives it no claim on the
         owner's photographs or documents. READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE is the same
         request on releases that predate the split, and is capped at API 32 so
         it is never asked for on a device that has the narrower permission.

         Nothing is uploaded, and no artwork is fetched unless the owner asks for
         the cover in an autofill. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_AUDIO"/>
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="32"/>

    <!-- Changing a track's tags, on Android 10 and earlier, and nothing else.
         On Android 7 to 10 it is granted together with the read access above,
         in the same system dialog: Android groups the two storage permissions,
         and the request for reading the library obtains this one as well. It
         is only ever used for an autofill the owner reviewed and confirmed,
         and a refusal writes nothing. On Android 11 and later the same write
         goes through all-files access below, asked for at the first confirmed
         autofill, and this is never asked for — which is why it is capped at
         API 29. What is written is the file's tags and nothing else: never
         its audio, its name or its place. -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="29"/>

    <!-- The memory card, and the drive plugged into the phone.

         Declared, and asked for at two moments only. The first: a folder the
         owner registered has been walked, has turned out to be unreadable, and
         the folders screen has said why and offered this as the way through.
         An owner whose music sits where Android expects it is never asked
         for that, and never has to be — READ_MEDIA_AUDIO above covers that
         storage on its own.

         What it exists for is the one thing that permission cannot reach.
         Android mounts a memory card and a USB drive on volumes outside the
         storage it hands an application by default, and there is no narrower
         permission for them: a folder on one either reads as a folder that is
         not there, or it reads with this.

         The second, on Android 11 and later: the owner has confirmed an
         autofill's changes, or started an autofill of many songs knowing its
         clear matches are written without review, and the application is
         about to change the tags of files it did not make. There is no
         narrower permission that lets an application do that there, and
         nothing is asked for before that.

         It changes nothing about what this application does with what it
         reads. Orpheus walks the folders the owner registered and no others,
         it opens audio files to read their tags and play them, and the only
         things it ever writes into a music folder are a .lrc beside a track
         whose words were looked up, and the tags of files whose autofill the
         owner confirmed or started. That is true with this permission exactly as it was
         without it. It can be taken back in the system settings at any
         time. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"/>

    <!-- The lyrics lookup, and the autofill lookup, and nothing else.

         This is the one permission here that reaches off the device, and it
         is worth being exact about what it is for. When a track has no words
         on this device — no .lrc beside it, no SYLT frame, no lyrics tag —
         and the owner has left the lookup on in the preferences, Orpheus asks
         a lyrics service for them using that track's artist and title. And
         when the owner starts an autofill, Orpheus asks MusicBrainz for the
         release, and the Cover Art Archive for its cover if the owner chose
         one.

         What the lyrics lookup sends is the artist and the title. What an
         autofill sends is the record's title and artist, or a lone track's
         title, artist and length. Not the file's name, not its path, not the
         library, not what else is on the device, not who is holding it, and
         nothing that persists between requests: no account, no key, no
         identifier of any kind. There is no telemetry, no
         analytics and no crash reporting in this package, and the absence of
         any other network call is checked in the same place this permission
         is: the workflow reads the shipped package's permissions back out of
         it and fails on anything not listed in this file.

         The owner can turn the lyrics lookup off, and an autofill is only
         ever made when the owner starts one; with both left alone this
         permission is declared and never exercised. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>

    <!-- Playing on after the owner switches away.

         A foreground service is the only way Android lets a process keep
         making noise once it is no longer on screen, and its type says what
         for: mediaPlayback, which is the one this application would qualify
         for and the one it asks for. WAKE_LOCK is what keeps the processor
         running through a track while the screen is off.

         POST_NOTIFICATIONS is the notification that comes with it, and is
         asked for at the moment there is something to show rather than on a
         launch nobody has pressed play in. A refusal costs the notification
         and nothing else — the service still runs, and so does the music.

         None of these is a network permission. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE"/>
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MEDIA_PLAYBACK"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WAKE_LOCK"/>

    <!-- requestLegacyExternalStorage: on Android 10 it is what lets the
         legacy write permission above reach a file by its path, since that
         release scopes storage unless it is asked to behave as the earlier
         ones did. It changes nothing on any other release. -->
    <application
        android:label="Orpheus"
        android:requestLegacyExternalStorage="true"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- The playback service, and the button events that reach it.

             `audio_service` supplies both; what is declared here is what the
             system needs to know about them. The service answers the media
             browser intent, which is what makes this application visible to
             anything that drives a player from outside it — the lock screen,
             a car head unit, a watch. The receiver is the media button: the
             pause on a pair of headphones, and the one on a steering wheel. -->
        <service
            android:name="com.ryanheise.audioservice.AudioService"
            android:foregroundServiceType="mediaPlayback"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.media.browse.MediaBrowserService"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <receiver
            android:name="com.ryanheise.audioservice.MediaButtonReceiver"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MEDIA_BUTTON"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
